(a) Statewide mandatory codes adopted. The following construction codes which have been made to apply throughout the State of Georgia and approved the Georgia Board of Community Affairs are hereby adopted and made applicable in Harris County to include any future editions or amendments as may be approved by the State of Georgia and/or the Georgia Department of Community Affairs.
(1) International Building Code, 2006 Edition;
(2) International Fuel Gas Code, 2006 Edition;
(3) International Mechanical Code, 2006 Edition;
(4) International Plumbing Code, 2006 Edition;
(5) National Electric Code NFPA 70, 2005 Edition;
(6) International Fire Code, 2006;
(7) International Energy Conservation Code, 2006 Edition;
(8) International Residential Code, 2006 Edition.
(b) Additional codes adopted. The following state minimum optional codes are adopted to include any future additional or amendments as may be approved by the State of Georgia or the Georgia Department of Community Development:
(1) International Property Maintenance Code, 2003 Edition;
(2) International Existing Building Code, 2003 Edition.
(c) Administration and enforcement of mandatory codes. Pursuant to Uniform Code Act (O.C.G.A. § 82-20 et seq.) specifically allowing local units of government to adopt reasonable administrative procedures for the local enforcement of the foregoing statewide mandatory codes in subsection 4-6(a), the following administrative enforcement procedures are adopted:
(1) International Building Code, 2006 Edition, chapter 1, Administration, except section 103, entitled "Department of Building Safety"; and section 112, entitled "Board of Appeals" which sections are not adopted herewith.
(2) International Fuel Gas Code, 2006 Edition, chapter 1, Administration, except section 103, entitled "Department of Inspection"; and section 109, entitled "Means of Appeal" which sections are not adopted herewith;
(3) International Mechanical Code, 2006 Edition, chapter 1, Administration, except section 103, entitled "Department of Mechanical Inspection"; and section 109, entitled "Means of Appeal" which sections are not adopted herewith;
(4) International Plumbing Code, 2006 Edition, chapter 1, Administration, except section 103 entitled, "Department of Plumbing Inspection"; and section 109, entitled "Means of Appeal" which sections are not adopted herewith;
(5) National Electric Code NFPA 70, 2005 Edition, except section 301, entitled "Department of Electrical Inspection" of chapter 3; and chapter 11, entitled "Means of Appeal"; and chapter 12, entitled "Administrative Provisions, 2006" which sections are not adopted herewith;
(6) International Fire Code, 2006, chapter 1, Administration, except section 103, entitled "Department of Fire Prevention"; and section 108, entitled "Board of Appeals" which sections are not adopted herewith;
(7) International Energy Conservation Code 2006 Edition, chapter 1, Administration;
(8) International Residential Code 2006, Edition, chapter 1, Administration, except section R103, entitled "Department of Building Safety"; and section R112, entitled "Board of Appeals" which sections are not adopted herewith.
(d) Administration and enforcement of additional codes. Pursuant to the Uniform Code Act (O.C.G.A. § 82-20 et. seq.) which specifically allows local units of government to adopt reasonable administrative procedures for the local enforcement of the foregoing additional state codes in subsection 4-6(b), the following administrative enforcement procedures sections are adopted:
(1) International Property Maintenance Code, 2003 Edition, chapter 1, Administration, except section 103, entitled "Department of Property Maintenance Inspection" and section 111, entitled "Means of Appeals," which sections are not adopted herewith.
(2) International Existing Building Code, 2006 Edition, chapter 1, Administration, except section 103, entitled "Department of Building Safety"; and section 112, entitled "Board of Appeals" which sections are not adopted herewith.
(e) Appendices. Appendix G to the International Residential Code, 2006 Edition is adopted except definitions of above-ground/on-ground pool, hot tub, spa, portable and that portion of the definition swimming pool designated as "above-ground and in-ground" and "hot tubs and spas" of section AG102.1-General; section AG103.2-Above-ground and Underground Swimming Pool; section AG104.1-Permanently Installed Spas and Hot Tubs; AG104.2-Portable Spas and Hot Tubs; numbered Paragraph 10 of section AG105.2-Outdoor Swimming Pool; section AG105.4-Prohibited Locations; section AG105.5-Barrier Exceptions, which sections are not adopted; attachment E4106.5.1 and attachment E4103.3 entitled Disconnecting Means for Pool Utilization Equipment of the National Electrical Code are adopted but modified so as to read "One or more means to disconnect all underground conductors for utilization equipment, other than lighting, shall be provided. Each of such means shall be readily accessible and within sight from the equipment it serves."
(f) Appeals. Board of construction appeals. All appeals under the foregoing mandatory codes and the additional codes shall be filed and determined as provided hereafter by the board of construction appeals, which shall consist of five members appointed by the county board of commissioners.
(1) Membership. The members of the board of construction appeals shall be composed of individuals with knowledge and experience in the construction codes. A board member shall not act in a case in which he has a personal or financial interest.
(2) Terms. The terms of office of the board member shall be staggered so no more than one-third of the board is appointed or replaced in any 12-month period. Vacancies shall be filled for an unexpired term in the manner in which original appointments are required to be made. Continued absence of any member from required meetings of the board shall, at the discretion of the county board of commissioners, render any such member subject to immediate removal from office.
(3) Quorum and voting. A simple majority of the board shall constitute a quorum. In varying any provision of the construction codes, the affirmative votes of the majority present shall be required. In modifying a decision of the building official, not less than two affirmative votes shall be required. In the event that regular members are unable to attend a meeting, the alternate members, if appointed, shall vote.
(4) Secretary of the board. The building official shall act as secretary of the board and shall make a record of all its proceedings, which shall set forth the reasons for its decision, the vote of each member, the absence of a member and any failure of a member to vote.
(5) Powers. The construction board of adjustments and appeals shall have the power, as further defined in section 4-5, to hear the appeals of decisions and interpretations of the building official and consider variances of the construction codes.
(6) Appeals of decisions of the building official. The owner of a building, structure or service system, or his duly authorized agent, may appeal a decision of the building official to the board of construction appeals when any one of the following conditions are claimed to exist:
a. The building official rejected or refused to approve the mode or manner of construction proposed to be followed or materials to be used in the installation or alteration of a building, structure or service system in contradiction or contravention of any adopted code.
b. The provisions of the construction codes do not apply to the specific case appealed.
c. The true intent and meaning of the construction codes or any of the regulations thereunder have been misconstrued or incorrectly interpreted.
(7) Variances. The board of construction appeals, when so appealed to and after a hearing, may reverse or alter the decision of the building official when, in its opinion, the enforcement thereof would do manifest injustice and would be contrary to the spirit and purpose of this or the construction codes or public interest, and the board as follows:
(a) That the decision of the building official is in contradiction or contravention of any adopted code.
(b) That the special conditions and circumstances appealed from do not result from the action or inaction of the applicant.
(c) That reversal or alteration will not confer on the applicant any special privilege that is denied by the construction codes to other buildings, structures or service system.
(d) That the grant of the relief sought by the appeal will be in harmony with the general intent and purpose of the construction codes and will not be detrimental to the public health, safety and general welfare.
(8) Notice of appeal. Notice of appeal shall be in writing and filed within 30 calendar days after the building official renders the decision. Appeals shall be in a form acceptable to the building official. The board shall meet within 30 calendar days after notice of appeal has been received or on call of the chairman.
(9) Unsafe or dangerous buildings or service systems. In the case of a building, structure, or service system, which, in the opinion of the building official, is unsafe, unsanitary or dangerous, the building official may, in his order, limit the time for such notice of appeals to a shorter period.
(10) Decisions. The board of construction appeals shall in every case reach a decision without unreasonable or unnecessary delay. Each decision of the board shall also include the reasons for the decision. If a decision of the board reverses or modifies a refusal, order, or disallowance of the building official, the building official shall immediately take action in accordance with such decision. Every decision shall be promptly filed in writing in the office of the building official and shall be open to public inspection. A certified copy of the decision shall be sent by mail to the appellant. Every decision of the board shall be final with no right of appeal to the county board of commissioners.
(Ord. No. 6-84, § 1, 11-6-84; Ord. No. 2-86, §§ 1, 4—7, 11-21-86; Ord. No. 3-86, §§ 1, 3, 12-16-86; Res. No. 4-91, 10-15-91; Ord. No. 1-95, 1-17-95; Ord. No. 1-96, 5-7-96; Ord. No. 2-97, 5-20-97; Ord. No. 08-07, § 1, 11-20-2007)

